                <pb facs="0536" n="498" sameAs="frus:frus1955-57v27/pg498"/>

                <head>177. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>The closing of the Suez Canal after Egyptian President Nasser’s nationalization
                    of the Suez Canal Company on July 26, 1956, adversely affected Denmark, Norway,
                    and Sweden; none had an indigenous oil supply and each was heavily dependent
                    upon oil for industry, agriculture, and domestic heating. Each government
                    requested the United States to cooperate in existing plans, put forward by the
                    Organization for European Economic Cooperation, for the allocation of oil in
                    Europe. Documentation on Scandinavian attitudes on this question is in
                    Department of State, BNA Files: Lot 59 D 21 and Lot 58 D 399. Norwegian Foreign
                    Minister Halvard Lange discussed the Middle East <pb facs="0537" n="499"
                        xml:id="pg499"/> situation and the oil problem with Acting Secretary Hoover
                    on November 30. A memorandum of their conversation is <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >ibid.,</hi> Central Files, 611.57/11–3056. Extensive documentation on the
                    crisis, including the question of possible oil shortages in Western Europe, is
                    in volume XVI.</p>
            </div>
